course: history
lesson: 55
title: China: the Song and the Mongols
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Under the Song dynasty, from 960 to 1279, China had huge cities, iron works, canals and the world's first government paper money, the jiaozi. Imagine paying for a bun with a printed note while most of Europe still paid in coins.
explain: Then came the Mongols. Genghis Khan's riders attacked northern China from 1211, and his grandson Kublai Khan finished the conquest in 1279 and founded the Yuan dynasty. Mei says «China absorbed the Mongols», the teacher says «the Mongols ruled China»: the Yuan was a Mongol dynasty ruling through a Chinese-style state.
explain: Mei also says the Mongols never lost a battle; ask Japan, where storms helped wreck two Mongol fleets, or Egypt, where the Mongols lost at Ain Jalut.
explain: The Yuan ended when floods, famine and rebellions shook it, and a former peasant and monk, Zhu Yuanzhang, took Beijing in 1368 and became the first Ming emperor.
fact: The Song dynasty, 960–1279, had huge cities, iron works, canals and the first government-issued paper money in the world, the jiaozi, in the early 11th century.
fact: The Song capitals Kaifeng and later Hangzhou may each have had over a million people, among the largest cities on Earth, while London had perhaps 20,000; Marco Polo called Hangzhou the finest city in the world.
fact: Foot binding, tightly wrapping girls' feet to keep them small, spread from the Song era; it caused lifelong pain and was banned in the early 20th century, and most Chinese today see it as a cruelty of the past.
fact: Genghis Khan's Mongols began attacking northern China in 1211, and his grandson Kublai Khan completed the conquest of the Song in 1279 and founded the Yuan dynasty.
fact: Marco Polo said he served Kublai Khan for 17 years; some doubted it because he did not mention the Great Wall or tea, but most historians think he really went, because details like salt and paper money fit Chinese records.
fact: Kublai Khan's fleets attacked Japan in 1274 and 1281 and failed, partly because of storms the Japanese called «kamikaze», divine wind; the Mongols also lost at Ain Jalut in 1260 and in Vietnam.
fact: Knowledge of gunpowder reached the Islamic world and Europe in the 13th century, likely through Mongol contacts and trade, though the exact route is debated.
fact: The Grand Canal links Beijing and Hangzhou, is about 1,800 km long, and its oldest parts are over 2,400 years old; it is the longest ancient canal.
fact: The Black Death of the 14th century probably started in Central Asia and spread along trade routes to Europe; China also suffered epidemics in that century.
fact: Floods, famine and Red Turban rebellions weakened the Yuan, and Zhu Yuanzhang, a former peasant and monk, took Beijing in 1368 and founded the Ming after years of war.
fact: Mongolia is an independent country that declared independence in 1911 and was recognised by China in 1946; Inner Mongolia is a region of China.
exercise: What was the jiaozi? | The first government paper money | A Song warship with paddle wheels | A kind of kite | A Mongol helmet
exercise: When did the Song dynasty rule? | 960–1279 | 618–907 | 1644–1912 | 221–206 BC
exercise: Which were Song capital cities? | Kaifeng and Hangzhou | Rome and Paris | Kyiv and Moscow | Beijing and Chang'an together
exercise: Who called Hangzhou the finest city in the world? | Marco Polo | Admiral Zheng He | Confucius himself | Genghis Khan
exercise: Why is foot binding seen today as a cruelty? | It caused girls lifelong pain | It made shoes too expensive for poor families to buy | It was only for soldiers | It made feet bigger
exercise: When was foot binding banned? | In the early 20th century | In 221 BC | In 1279, by order of Kublai Khan | It was never banned
exercise: Who founded the Yuan dynasty? | Kublai Khan | Zhu Yuanzhang | Qin Shi Huang | Sun Yat-sen in 1912
exercise: In which year did the Mongols complete the conquest of the Song? | 1279 | 1066 | 1911 | 1453
exercise: What details suggest Marco Polo really went to China? | His descriptions of salt and paper money fit Chinese records | He brought back a brick from the Great Wall to show the people of Venice | He took photos | He wrote his whole book in Chinese characters
exercise: What does «kamikaze» mean originally? | Divine wind | Fast fighting ship | Brave soldier | Red rising sun
exercise: Where did the Mongols lose a battle in 1260? | Ain Jalut | Hastings in England | Waterloo in Belgium | The Talas river
exercise: How did gunpowder probably reach Europe? | Through Mongol contacts and trade | By a Japanese ship | By old Roman roads | It was invented in Europe first, long before China
exercise: Which two cities does the Grand Canal link? | Beijing and Hangzhou | Beijing and Moscow | Xi'an and Rome | Shanghai and Tokyo
exercise: Where do scientists think the Black Death started? | Central Asia | Western Europe | South America | Northern Australia
exercise: What was Zhu Yuanzhang before he became emperor? | A peasant and a monk | A Mongol prince of the Yuan | A British sailor | A Tang poet
exercise: What is Mongolia today? | An independent country | A province of China ruled from Beijing | Part of Japan | Part of Russia
test: Mei says the Song had the first factories. What is the more exact claim? | Large workshops, iron works and the first paper money | Car factories | No workshops at all | Factories with robots and moving belts, just like today
test: The teacher compares Hangzhou with London at that time. What does the gap teach? | Song cities were far bigger than European ones then | London was much bigger, with more than a million people | Both were tiny villages | Hangzhou was empty
test: Mei repeats from an old drama that foot binding was beautiful. What would her mum and the teacher say? | It was a harmful custom that hurt girls; old customs can be wrong | It was healthy exercise that the doctors told every single family to do | It made girls run faster | It is still the law
test: Mei says «China absorbed the Mongols», the teacher «the Mongols ruled China». Wisest? | A Mongol dynasty ruling China through a Chinese-style state | Only Mei is right | China conquered Mongolia and made it a province ruled from the south | The Mongols never came
test: dude says marco polo never went to china cos he didn't mention the wall. convinced? | Most historians think he went; his details fit Chinese records | Yes, it is proven he stayed at home in Venice the whole time and made it up | He went to Japan instead | He invented China
test: Mei says the Mongols never lost a battle. What would the teacher list? | Japan in 1274 and 1281, Ain Jalut and Vietnam | Nothing; she is right | Only Waterloo | Only the Great Wall, where the Ming stopped them
test: If storms had not hit the Mongol fleets, what might have changed? | The invasions of Japan might have gone differently | Gunpowder would vanish | The Song would rule Japan | Nothing could change
test: The teacher says the plague came from China, Mei says from Europe. What do scientists now say? | It probably started in Central Asia | Definitely from Europe | Definitely from America | From the Moon
test: Mei says the Chinese drove out the Mongols in one day. What is true? | It took years of war after floods, famine and rebellions | It took one afternoon | The Mongols are still in charge | The Romans drove them out
test: Mei points at the map: «Mongolia is part of China». What did she mix up? | Mongolia, a country, and Inner Mongolia, a region of China | Mongolia and Japan | Mongolia and Tibet | Nothing; she is right
test: Why would a canal linking the north and south be so useful in China? | It moves grain and goods along the country | It keeps out enemies | It makes rain | It is only for fishing
exam: Identify the institution which renders the Song dynasty notable in the history of currency. | The first paper money issued by a government, the jiaozi | The first gold coin bearing a portrait of the reigning emperor | The abolition of every form of money | The adoption of Spanish silver dollars
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the foundation of the Yuan dynasty. | It was established by Kublai Khan upon completion of the conquest of the Song in 1279 | It was founded by Zhu Yuanzhang in 1368 | It was proclaimed by Qin Shi Huang in 221 BC | It was founded by the Manchus in 1644
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the present scholarly view of Marco Polo's account? | Most historians accept that he went, his details corresponding to Chinese records | It is universally held to be a fabrication | It is known to describe Japan rather than China | It was composed in Chinese by Kublai Khan
exam: Ascertain the original signification of the Japanese term kamikaze. | Divine wind | Imperial fleet | The rising sun | Brave warrior
